[{:db/id 17592186198454 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cf616da5-d6d0-5837-a9fb-64c2cf137040" "question-8a69da2c-f673-5e64-a265-9fe904a3c9ad"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf616da5-d6d0-5837-a9fb-64c2cf137040" :question/id #uuid "cf616da5-d6d0-5837-a9fb-64c2cf137040" :question/math-academy-id "q-40488" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ac075b6f-3299-5310-ad52-26f9cf648385"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ac075b6f-3299-5310-ad52-26f9cf648385" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac075b6f-3299-5310-ad52-26f9cf648385" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-33d47db5-c434-55d6-9456-167ab8a0b67c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7c3a74ea-6a6d-5da3-b53b-157fcb966ead" "answer-b81c5bc9-bfe5-5ce5-928d-fa684e207472" "answer-f3f62bc1-8a13-5efa-ad9a-759018432b14" "answer-33d47db5-c434-55d6-9456-167ab8a0b67c" "answer-c6a8591a-2779-5016-a0d3-d51746099b13"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c3a74ea-6a6d-5da3-b53b-157fcb966ead" :answer/id #uuid "7c3a74ea-6a6d-5da3-b53b-157fcb966ead" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b81c5bc9-bfe5-5ce5-928d-fa684e207472" :answer/id #uuid "b81c5bc9-bfe5-5ce5-928d-fa684e207472" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3f62bc1-8a13-5efa-ad9a-759018432b14" :answer/id #uuid "f3f62bc1-8a13-5efa-ad9a-759018432b14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33d47db5-c434-55d6-9456-167ab8a0b67c" :answer/id #uuid "33d47db5-c434-55d6-9456-167ab8a0b67c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6a8591a-2779-5016-a0d3-d51746099b13" :answer/id #uuid "c6a8591a-2779-5016-a0d3-d51746099b13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198455 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9892c6c7-24de-57f2-a3e8-0b32de0c1349"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9892c6c7-24de-57f2-a3e8-0b32de0c1349" :question/id #uuid "9892c6c7-24de-57f2-a3e8-0b32de0c1349" :question/math-academy-id "q-40491" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\n- $U={Q([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])}$\n- $W={Q([\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])}$\n- $V={Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})},\\,$ ${Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})},\\,$ and $\\,{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})}.$ \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=18-18=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=1-1=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-90932faf-686a-5262-b048-f69de0f0ccfb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-90932faf-686a-5262-b048-f69de0f0ccfb" :answer-field/id #uuid "90932faf-686a-5262-b048-f69de0f0ccfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0dfd22c3-475e-57e7-a43b-2426876d96a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2e6fe6e-e075-5371-baff-975eb86d75ba" "answer-0dfd22c3-475e-57e7-a43b-2426876d96a9" "answer-18261590-5d63-59fa-a5a2-24d4904d43be" "answer-25feb7c8-dd5c-59a1-bec8-c633a782af5e" "answer-2eca64ea-f21c-59ab-b00b-5d3ef14bd0fb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2e6fe6e-e075-5371-baff-975eb86d75ba" :answer/id #uuid "d2e6fe6e-e075-5371-baff-975eb86d75ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0dfd22c3-475e-57e7-a43b-2426876d96a9" :answer/id #uuid "0dfd22c3-475e-57e7-a43b-2426876d96a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18261590-5d63-59fa-a5a2-24d4904d43be" :answer/id #uuid "18261590-5d63-59fa-a5a2-24d4904d43be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25feb7c8-dd5c-59a1-bec8-c633a782af5e" :answer/id #uuid "25feb7c8-dd5c-59a1-bec8-c633a782af5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2eca64ea-f21c-59ab-b00b-5d3ef14bd0fb" :answer/id #uuid "2eca64ea-f21c-59ab-b00b-5d3ef14bd0fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8a69da2c-f673-5e64-a265-9fe904a3c9ad" :question/id #uuid "8a69da2c-f673-5e64-a265-9fe904a3c9ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-40514" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-491c6e1c-81dd-5170-b618-833ce2679d93"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-491c6e1c-81dd-5170-b618-833ce2679d93" :answer-field/id #uuid "491c6e1c-81dd-5170-b618-833ce2679d93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b3565871-fc75-5a3f-bb6e-506cbb0458f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24e42699-a0f0-5e81-a194-c91514b7e8ae" "answer-bfd57b0f-3965-5b70-833f-7fa36e06ae4f" "answer-b3565871-fc75-5a3f-bb6e-506cbb0458f5" "answer-46c27bd0-b639-51d1-a04d-8098da45991e" "answer-e14e0ad1-a7b2-5023-b79c-bff39fbd35df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24e42699-a0f0-5e81-a194-c91514b7e8ae" :answer/id #uuid "24e42699-a0f0-5e81-a194-c91514b7e8ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfd57b0f-3965-5b70-833f-7fa36e06ae4f" :answer/id #uuid "bfd57b0f-3965-5b70-833f-7fa36e06ae4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3565871-fc75-5a3f-bb6e-506cbb0458f5" :answer/id #uuid "b3565871-fc75-5a3f-bb6e-506cbb0458f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46c27bd0-b639-51d1-a04d-8098da45991e" :answer/id #uuid "46c27bd0-b639-51d1-a04d-8098da45991e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e14e0ad1-a7b2-5023-b79c-bff39fbd35df" :answer/id #uuid "e14e0ad1-a7b2-5023-b79c-bff39fbd35df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-11d38e3f-6339-5429-8d89-8272fca9a35c" :activity/id #uuid "11d38e3f-6339-5429-8d89-8272fca9a35c" :activity/title "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050916 :activity/course 175921860466